{
  // Vaipakam first-party autonomous keeper Worker.
  //
  // Cron-driven only (no fetch handler). Stage 3 PR2 of the
  // Worker split — see docs/DesignsAndPlans/Stage3WorkerSplitPlan.md
  // for the full classification and migration sequencing.
  //
  // Owns: HF watcher loop + autonomous liquidation submission +
  // HF-band-downgrade Telegram / Push alerts + daily oracle
  // snapshot signer (moved from agent in the Stage 3 architectural-
  // rebalance commit; co-located with the existing
  // KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY consumer so the signing key lives on
  // exactly one Worker, per staging plan §2). LIVE (per §7 of the Stage 3
  // plan; this said "Future scope"): the off-chain offer matcher runs
  // every cron tick and signs matchOffers/matchIntent.
  //
  // Distinct from the public reference keeper-bot at the sibling
  // `vaipakam-keeper-bot` repo (different deploy target, different
  // trust assumptions). That repo follows its own ABI sync + CI
  // workflow per the Phase 9.A docs in CLAUDE.md.
  //
  // Custom domain: not user-facing. Worker name `vaipakam-keeper`
  // is the deploy-switch identifier and stays post-cutover.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "vaipakam-keeper",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-04-23",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // ── keep_vars: the dashboard's plain-text vars survive a deploy ──
  //
  // Wrangler DELETES every var not present in this file before setting the
  // ones that are, so any value managed only in the dashboard is wiped by an
  // ordinary `wrangler deploy`. That is the hazard `--keep-vars` was being
  // added by hand at every call site to avoid — across runbooks, deploy
  // scripts, workflows and package scripts — which is an unbounded set of
  // spellings to keep correct, and #1995 spent 242 review findings proving it.
  //
  // Set HERE instead, the preservation is a property of the WORKER rather than
  // of each invocation: wrangler reads `config.keep_vars` on both the deploy
  // path and `versions upload`, so every spelling becomes safe at once,
  // including the ones nobody has written yet.
  //
  // Declared vars are still uploaded normally — this only suppresses the
  // DELETE of bindings absent from the config (wire field
  // `keep_bindings: ["plain_text","json"]`). Secrets were never at risk;
  // wrangler keeps those unconditionally.
  //
  // CONSEQUENCE, and it is the intended trade: a deploy can no longer REMOVE
  // a var. Deleting one is now a deliberate dashboard action.
  "keep_vars": true,

  // ── KEEPER IS DEPLOYED BUT NOT SCHEDULED (#1896, operator decision) ──
  //
  // The schedule is deliberately EMPTY. Everything else about this
  // Worker is untouched and stays deployed: the script, the D1 binding,
  // all 17 secret / secrets-store / plain-text bindings, and the
  // `KEEPER_ENABLED` secret. Nothing has to be recreated to bring it
  // back — only this list.
  //
  // WHY. Measured on 2026-08-24 (#1896): `outcome: exceededCpu` on
  // ~100% of invocations, cpuTime pinned at the 10 ms ceiling, over the
  // whole retained window. So every minute the Worker was invoked,
  // burned its ceiling, and was terminated rather than finishing.
  //
  // NOT "completed nothing" — an earlier revision said that and the
  // release note was corrected while this comment was not (Codex #1924
  // r3). Passes run concurrently, and several DO emit their completion
  // line before another exhausts the invocation; in an armed
  // configuration those may have sent alerts or written records first.
  // What is certain is the termination, not an absence of work.
  //
  // WHICH pass burns it is NOT established, and an earlier revision of
  // this comment claimed it was (Codex #1924 r1). `watcher` and
  // `liquidityConfidence` are the two with no completion line, but
  // every due pass is started concurrently through `ctx.waitUntil`, so
  // a missing line means only "still in flight when the invocation
  // died" — which a pass merely awaiting an RPC also satisfies. The
  // timing wrapper measures WALL time and says so itself: it can
  // EXCLUDE suspects, not name one. Attribution needs a profile.
  //
  // It also returns a cron trigger to the account pool. The free plan
  // caps triggers at FIVE per ACCOUNT and the pool was full — see
  // `apps/indexer/src/cronRouting.ts`, which records a deploy that
  // failed for exactly that reason, and
  // `docs/ops/CloudflareCronSlots.md` for what the pool holds today.
  //
  // WHAT STOPS THAT WAS NOT ALREADY STOPPED. FOUR passes run ungated by
  // `KEEPER_ENABLED`, so unscheduling does cost function. Two earlier
  // revisions of this comment got this wrong in escalating ways — first
  // claiming it cost nothing, then naming only two of the four (Codex
  // #1924 r1/r2). README §"What the kill-switch does and does not stop
  // (#1548)" is the authority and lists all of them:
  //   - `watcher` — THE BIGGEST LOSS, and the one both earlier revisions
  //     missed. It keeps evaluating positions and keeps sending Telegram
  //     and Push health-factor alerts to USERS. Unscheduling stops those
  //     alerts: a borrower approaching liquidation is no longer told.
  //   - `dailyOracleSnapshot` — gated only on KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY, and
  //     deliberately so: the same README section records the 2026-08-03
  //     decision that the snapshot is a public good, left ungated
  //     precisely SO THAT disabling the keeper for an unrelated reason
  //     would not leave holes in the oracle series. Unscheduling leaves
  //     exactly those holes, and they do NOT backfill on re-enable — the
  //     contract writes only the current day's slot. Mitigation:
  //     `captureDailyPriceSnapshot` is permissionlessly callable, so any
  //     address can keep the series intact meanwhile.
  //   - `preGraceWatcher` — no arming check at all; borrowers stop
  //     getting the pre-grace warning.
  //   - `liquidityConfidence` — its gate is narrower than it looks: it
  //     decides only whether to SUBMIT on-chain. The pass still reads
  //     and still writes its D1 counter, so unscheduling stops the
  //     confidence state advancing at all.
  //
  // THERE IS NO WORKER RE-ENABLE ANY MORE (#2496; owner decision
  // 2026-10-15). All keeper runs moved to the always-on machine
  // (docs/ops/KeeperMachine.md), and this Worker's `scheduled` handler
  // declines every invocation by name (`src/index.ts`), so restoring a cron
  // here runs nothing. Keep `"crons": []`. The Worker stays deployed only as
  // the private database's migration applier (`run deploy`). The procedure
  // that used to stand here re-scheduled the Worker; it is retired with it,
  // and its history is in git (#1896, #1924, #1978).
  //
  // The original cadence note, still true and still the target to
  // restore: the watcher is internally tick-rate-aware — each tick
  // sweeps `getChainConfigs(env)` round-robin and bails early on chains
  // that already have notify_state at the same band since the previous
  // tick. "* * * * *" matched the historical ops/hf-watcher cadence;
  // tighter schedules don't help (HF reads are per-tick rate-limited
  // inside the watcher).
  "triggers": {
    "crons": [],
  },

  // D1 binding — `vaipakam-warm`, the STAGING database for the
  // 3-Worker split. NOT `vaipakam-alerts-db` (primary/production),
  // which this comment used to name — that misdirects migration and
  // incident work at production. The binding below is authoritative.
  //
  // All three Workers bind this same database id and all three WRITE
  // it. A D1 binding is database-scoped, not table-scoped, so the
  // per-Worker table lists below are a source convention, not an
  // enforced boundary (#1722). Tables this Worker touches:
  //   - `user_thresholds`   (per-user-loan HF bands; written by frontend)
  //   - `telegram_links`    (Telegram handshake links; agent writes — but
  //                          THIS Worker deletes from them too:
  //                          `watcher.ts:60` calls `sweepExpiredLinks`,
  //                          which DELETEs expired rows (`db.ts:303`).
  //                          "agent writes" alone read as though the keeper
  //                          only consumed this table)
  //   - `notify_state`      (per-user notify dedupe)
  //   - `pre_grace_notify_state` (pre-grace warning dedupe; read and
  //                          written by the scheduled `runPreGraceWatcher`)
  //   - `indexer_cursor`    (READ ONLY — `recordHfBandNotifications`
  //                          reads the indexer's `notified` watermark and
  //                          `diamond` tip before materializing rows, so a
  //                          cron sentinel can't out-sort still-pending
  //                          event rows. The indexer owns the writes)
  //   - `notifications`     (HF-band downgrade rows, INSERT OR IGNORE on
  //                          the dedup key; same call path, reached from
  //                          the scheduled `runLiquidator`)
  //   - `loans`             (active-loan rows; written by indexer; this
  //                          Worker reads to enumerate live loans)
  //   - `offers`            (read by dailyOracleSnapshot; NOT by the
  //                          matcher, whose discovery is on-chain)
  //   - `oracle_snapshot_state` (daily snapshot dedupe)
  //   - `liquidity_confidence` (liquidity probe)
  //   - `keeper_commitment_reconciled` (reconciled-day rediscovery; the
  //                          indexer writes it, so it stays shared)
  //   - `push_rail_status`  (WRITE, #2667 — this keeper's Push rail
  //                          readiness, one row replaced on every watcher
  //                          run; shared because the agent reads it)
  //
  // The keeper's OWN operational tables — `hf_band_state`,
  // `keeper_commitment_scan`, `keeper_commitment_day`,
  // `keeper_remit_ack_frontier`, `keeper_remit_ack` — are NOT here any more: they live behind `KEEPER_DB` below (#2450).
  //
  // Names verified against apps/indexer/migrations/ (and, for the KEEPER_DB
  // tables, apps/keeper/migrations/). `thresholds`,
  // `link_codes` and `loan_index_*` were named here and exist under no
  // migration. The three entries above them were verified the other way
  // round — from the live call graph rather than from this list — because
  // a table this Worker touches but does not name is the failure mode a
  // name check cannot catch: an auditor reading only the binding comment
  // would conclude the keeper never writes the user-facing `notifications`
  // inbox, and a migration author would not know to check it.
  // Cross-Worker D1 sharing is the deliberate coupling that lets the
  // three Workers stay independent without a message-bus layer
  // (Stage 3 plan §5).
  // D1 binding — `vaipakam-warm` is the STAGING database for
  // the new 3-Worker split (apps/{keeper,indexer,agent}). It's
  // distinct from `vaipakam-alerts-db` which the legacy
  // `vaipakam-hf-watcher` Worker (primary infra) writes to and
  // is intentionally untouched until staging is fully validated.
  // See docs/DesignsAndPlans/CloudflareStagingDeployPlan.md §3
  // for the staging-vs-primary D1 split.
  //
  // TWO databases since #2450, split by who may read the data:
  //
  //   - `DB` — the SHARED `vaipakam-warm`, for every table another Worker,
  //     the app or the nightly backup also reads. Its schema is
  //     apps/indexer's, which is why its `migrations_dir` names that
  //     directory explicitly: wrangler defaults every entry to `./migrations`,
  //     so without it a `wrangler d1 migrations apply vaipakam-warm` typed in
  //     THIS directory would apply the keeper's private DDL to the shared
  //     database. Named, the same command applies the indexer's own files —
  //     the ones that database already records.
  //   - `KEEPER_DB` — `vaipakam-keeper-db`, the keeper's PRIVATE operational
  //     state (scan cursors, completion markers, ack log, HF-band edge state,
  //     its redeploy epoch). Nothing outside this Worker reads it, and the
  //     indexer and agent have no binding to it, so neither can write the
  //     keeper's completion records. Separate by trust boundary, the rule
  //     ops/mesh-watcher already follows (CLAUDE.md, "Cloudflare D1 schema
  //     discipline"). Schema in apps/keeper/migrations/, applied by the keeper's own
  //     `deploy` (scripts/deploy-keeper.mjs runs `run migrate` first, then
  //     verifies) — the keeper is that database's one applier — or alone with
  //     `pnpm --filter @vaipakam/keeper run migrate`.
  //
  // A maintenance build (#2239) omits BOTH entries: the barrier is an empty
  // `d1_databases`, and the tick declines while either binding is absent.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "vaipakam-warm",
      "database_id": "e5e927cf-56c3-42c7-9820-179a235cc84f",
      "migrations_dir": "../indexer/migrations",
    },
    {
      "binding": "KEEPER_DB",
      "database_name": "vaipakam-keeper-db",
      "database_id": "f809ca7a-bc4a-4b17-b5bd-f36c3a3e9095",
      "migrations_dir": "migrations",
    },
  ],

  // T-078 — the keeper's secrets are read from the account-level
  // Cloudflare Secrets Store (store `vaipakam-credentials`, id
  // 1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e), not per-Worker
  // `wrangler secret put`. Each binding is resolved asynchronously
  // once per cron tick in `resolveEnv()` (src/env.ts). Local
  // `wrangler dev` needs the same secrets created WITHOUT the
  // `--remote` flag.
  //
  // `RPC_ZKEVM` is omitted (Polygon zkEVM out of scope); `RPC_POLYGON`
  // is agent-only. (#1651: this said `RPC_POLYGON_AMOY` was agent-only
  // too — apps/indexer binds it.) `TG_BOT_TOKEN` must still be
  // created in the store (the `VaipakamBot` token) before deploy.
  "secrets_store_secrets": [
    { "binding": "RPC_BASE",          "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BASE" },
    { "binding": "RPC_ETH",           "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_ETH" },
    { "binding": "RPC_ARB",           "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_ARB" },
    { "binding": "RPC_OP",            "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_OP" },
    { "binding": "RPC_BNB",           "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BNB" },
    { "binding": "RPC_SEPOLIA",       "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_BASE_SEPOLIA",  "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BASE_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_ARB_SEPOLIA",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_ARB_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_OP_SEPOLIA",    "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_OP_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_BNB_TESTNET",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BNB_TESTNET" },
    { "binding": "TG_BOT_TOKEN",      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "TG_BOT_TOKEN" },
    { "binding": "PUSH_CHANNEL_PK",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "PUSH_CHANNEL_PK" },
    { "binding": "ZEROEX_API_KEY",    "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "ZEROEX_API_KEY" },
    { "binding": "ONEINCH_API_KEY",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "ONEINCH_API_KEY" },
    { "binding": "KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY", "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY" },
  ],

  // Operator-managed config. TWO CLASSES, and this block used to state
  // only one — it described all of the below as "non-secret config — plain
  // `vars`", which is wrong for the arming flags and is the wording
  // `docs/ops/OffChainRestore.md` names as the incorrect description (§1
  // step 6f for capture, revisited in §7a when re-arming — NOT §6, which is
  // the indexer re-bootstrap).
  // #2223 corrected this block and every other site it found saying
  // otherwise — the README, the root `CLAUDE.md`, `src/env.ts`'s own
  // passthrough label, `SecretsStoreMigration.md`, the incident runbook's
  // remittance prerequisite, and several now-stale warnings that this
  // comment could not be trusted.
  //
  // That list is what was found and fixed. It is deliberately NOT a claim
  // that no other prose anywhere says something looser: three review rounds
  // each produced one more, because "no document could mislead a reader
  // about this" is not a checkable property.
  //
  // On a disagreement, resolve it against the LIVE DEPLOYMENT and the
  // recorded decision — `docs/ops/OffChainRestore.md` §7a carries the
  // 2026-07-30 readback, and #1465 / #2223 carry the do-not-commit decision.
  // NOT against this comment or `src/env.ts`: both are descriptions, and
  // `BaseEnv` types a var and a `secret_text` identically, so the code
  // cannot establish the binding class at all. If a ratified decision ever
  // changes the intended classification, these comments are what gets
  // updated — they do not get to outrank it.
  //
  // (a) ARMING FLAGS — `secret_text`, set with `wrangler secret put`, NOT
  //     entries in the `vars` block below. Verified against the live
  //     `vaipakam-keeper` deployment on 2026-07-30 for KEEPER_ENABLED; the
  //     two REWARD_* flags are ABSENT there, which leaves THREE scheduled
  //     passes dark, not two — rewardBudgetRemit and remitAck both gate on
  //     REWARD_REMIT_ENABLED, commitmentReport on REWARD_COMMIT_ENABLED —
  //     and are held the same way once armed. GRACE_DEFAULT_ENABLED (#2483)
  //     ships unset too, which keeps graceDefault dark: a FOURTH. Their VALUES cannot be read
  //     back from the API or dashboard, which is why each gated pass
  //     reports how it resolved them (#1475).
  //
  //     DO NOT move these into `vars` to make the arming state reviewable.
  //     It is a real temptation — the live value is otherwise invisible —
  //     but it changes deployment semantics twice over. A committed var arms
  //     (or disarms) the keeper from any clean checkout. And the var does not
  //     sit BESIDE the secret: wrangler's collision check warns it "will
  //     replace these remote secrets with the configuration values", so the
  //     commit CONVERTS the binding and the secret is gone — there is no
  //     second binding to fall back to, and no rollback short of setting the
  //     secret again after deleting the var. `src/env.ts` records the
  //     decision; argue with it there, not around it here.
  //
  //   - KEEPER_ENABLED   — 'true' arms the autonomous liquidation /
  //                        matcher / liquidity-confidence submit paths,
  //                        and gates the REWARD_* and GRACE_DEFAULT flags
  //                        below as well.
  //   - REWARD_REMIT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #776 reward-budget remit pass
  //     (only after the keeper EOA is authorized on-chain) AND the #1222
  //     B2-d2 remit-ACK pass (delivered-backing reservations; its ack tables
  //     moved from shared migration 0044 to the keeper's private
  //     `vaipakam-keeper-db`, #2450, whose schema the keeper's deploy applies).
  //   - REWARD_COMMIT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #1222 B2-d1 mirror→Base
  //     commitment-report pass (mirror chains only; keeper EOA needs on-chain
  //     KEEPER_ROLE).
  //   - REWARD_SANCTIONS_KEEPER_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2450 sanctions-hold
  //     observer (every reward chain; its ops-alert table is in the keeper's
  //     private `vaipakam-keeper-db`).
  //   - REWARD_DAY_CLOSE_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2612 reward day-close pass
  //     (every reward chain: closeDay; on Base also finalizeDay and
  //     broadcastGlobal; it pays the quoted CCIP fees from the keeper key).
  //   - REWARD_HORIZON_HEARTBEAT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2517 reward
  //     claim-horizon heartbeat (every reward chain; its entry queue and
  //     cursor are in the keeper's private `vaipakam-keeper-db`). Arm it on a
  //     chain BEFORE `setRewardClaimHorizonDays` (GovernanceRunbook, M7.2).
  //   - REWARD_ENTRY_SETTLE_ENABLED — 'true' adds the #2984 entry close-out
  //     to the armed heartbeat: retiring closed entries whose remaining window
  //     prices to nothing, and sweeping forfeited entries into the recycle
  //     bucket. Permissionless; no fee, nothing to the keeper. Ships unset.
  //     All three added after the 2026-07-30 readback above, so that
  //     readback says nothing about them.
  //
  //   The ops alert channel those passes report to — TG_OPS_BOT_TOKEN and
  //   TG_OPS_CHAT_ID — is likewise a secret / machine env value (the delivery
  //   ops/mesh-watcher uses), never a `vars` entry and never the user-facing
  //   TG_BOT_TOKEN.
  //   - GRACE_DEFAULT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2483 time-based default
  //     pass, which submits the permissionless `triggerDefault` for
  //     past-grace loans and so sells or transfers the borrower's
  //     collateral. Needs no on-chain role. Arming it is an owner decision.
  //   - PERIODIC_SETTLE_ENABLED — 'true' arms the periodic-interest settler,
  //     which submits the permissionless `settlePeriodicInterest` once a
  //     period's grace is over and so sells part of the borrower's
  //     collateral when the period's interest is unpaid. Ships unset. Needs
  //     no on-chain role. Arming it is an owner decision.
  //   - REWARD_CLOSE_OUT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2984 reward close-out
  //     pass: day-closure walks, retired-era finalization, the pending-surplus
  //     drain, the transport-remainder park and the day-cursor prune. All
  //     permissionless; it pays no fee and moves nothing to the keeper. Ships
  //     unset. Needs no on-chain role. Arming it is an owner decision.
  //   - REWARD_ZEROED_DAY_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2984 reward zeroed-day
  //     pass: on a mirror, each deliberately zeroed day's compensation quote
  //     (it pays the quote's CCIP fee) and the two lapse terminals. All
  //     permissionless. Ships unset. Needs no on-chain role. Arming it is an
  //     owner decision.
  //   - REWARD_RETURN_TRANSPORT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2984 reward
  //     return-transport pass: repatriation instructions, executions and
  //     cancellation ACKs, stranded-compensation returns and a retired
  //     mirror's surplus return. All permissionless; every send pays its CCIP
  //     fee. Ships unset. Needs no on-chain role. Arming it is an owner
  //     decision.
  //   - PROTOCOL_UPKEEP_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2984 protocol upkeep
  //     pass: the expired-buyback-intent rollback and the tier LTV cache
  //     refresh. Both permissionless; it pays no fee and moves nothing to the
  //     keeper. Ships unset. Needs no on-chain role. Arming it is an owner
  //     decision.
  //   - PREPAY_SALE_SANCTIONS_SYNC_ENABLED — 'true' adds the #2984 prepay-sale
  //     sanctions sync to the armed upkeep pass: syncPrepaySaleListing /
  //     syncPrepaySaleOffer for a live listing with a flagged party.
  //     Permissionless; no fee, nothing to the keeper. Ships unset.
  //   - TIER_PUSH_RETRY_ENABLED — 'true' adds the #2984 tier-push retry to the
  //     armed upkeep pass: deliverTierPush for a user whose tier push a lane
  //     is still owed, when the protocol broadcast budget can pay it. The
  //     lane's CCIP fee comes from that budget; the keeper attaches no value
  //     and receives none. Ships unset.
  //   - PREPAY_AUTO_LIST_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #2984 grace auto-list
  //     pass: autoListAtFloorOnGrace for NFT-collateral loans in grace, every
  //     minute. Permissionless; it pays only gas and moves nothing to the
  //     keeper. Ships unset. Needs no on-chain role. Arming it is an owner
  //     decision.
  //
  // (b) GENUINE PLAIN VARS. `keep_vars: true` preserves the ones this config
  //     does NOT declare — it suppresses the delete of bindings absent from
  //     the config, and declared vars are still uploaded normally (see the
  //     deployment-mechanics comment above). So the promise is scoped:
  //
  //     DASHBOARD-OWNED (omitted here, therefore preserved). Losing one does
  //     not turn a duty off the way (a) does — but do NOT read that as "any
  //     default is fine", which this comment said until #2223:
  //   - LIQ_* / SPLIT_* / PARTIAL_LIQ_* tuning knobs (see src/env.ts) —
  //     all optional, sensible defaults. These are the ones that genuinely
  //     just degrade tuning.
  //   - The REWARD_* knobs below are NOT in that class on a deployment that
  //     has tuned them. A reset REWARD_REMIT_LANE_CAP changes the size of
  //     each remitted part (#918: the pass also bounds each part by the
  //     lane bucket's live tokens, so it no longer makes sends revert); a
  //     reset REWARD_REMIT_LOOKBACK_DAYS can drop an old un-remitted day out
  //     of the re-scan window, which then needs a widened window or the
  //     manual remit procedure (IncidentRunbook §2b).
  //   - FRONTEND_ORIGIN — optional but WITHOUT a usable default, so it does
  //     not belong with the knobs above: unset, consumers fall back to the
  //     empty string and a notification deep link renders as the relative
  //     `/loans/{id}`, which is not clickable from Telegram or Push. Set it
  //     on any deploy whose notifications are meant to be followed.
  //   - REWARD_REMIT_LOOKBACK_DAYS (default 45), REWARD_REMIT_LANE_CAP
  //     (wei, default 50000e18 = the on-chain lane default; the ceiling on
  //     each remitted part, #918), REWARD_REMIT_MIN_PART (wei, default
  //     1000e18; the smallest part that may leave its day owing, #2960),
  //     REWARD_COMMIT_LOOKBACK_DAYS (default 14), REWARD_DAY_CLOSE_MAX_FEE_WEI
  //     (per-chain CCIP fee ceiling per send, default 0.002 native; #2627),
  //     REWARD_CLOSE_OUT_WINDOW_DAYS (default 60; #2984),
  //     REWARD_ZEROED_DAY_WINDOW_DAYS (default 60; #2984),
  //     REWARD_ZEROED_DAY_MAX_FEE_WEI (its CCIP fee ceiling per send; #2984),
  //     REWARD_RETURN_TRANSPORT_MAX_FEE_WEI (its CCIP fee ceiling per send;
  //     #2984), REWARD_RETURN_MIN_PART (default 1000e18; #2984),
  //     TIER_LTV_REFRESH_AFTER_SEC (default 604800 = 7 days; #2984)
  //     — knobs for (a)'s passes,
  //     but plain vars themselves.
  //
  //     CONFIG-OWNED (declared below, therefore NOT preserved — every deploy
  //     uploads the declared value and overwrites a dashboard edit):
  //   - TG_BOT_USERNAME  — public Telegram bot handle, and LEGACY: no code
  //                        on this Worker reads it (it is declared, typed and
  //                        passed through, and nothing consumes it). The
  //                        Telegram link that uses the handle is built by
  //                        apps/agent from ITS binding, which a keeper deploy
  //                        does not touch — so changing this value here
  //                        changes nothing observable. It stays because the
  //                        declaration is what makes the config-owned vs
  //                        dashboard-owned split above concrete.
  "vars": {
    // Public Telegram bot handle (the @-name from BotFather). Filled
    // post-provision; the worker accepts an empty string and returns
    // bot_url: null in the handshake response when not configured.
    "TG_BOT_USERNAME": "",
    // #2503 — this runner's name for the one-runner lease. A machine host
    // uses its own name, so the Worker and a machine can never both run.
    "KEEPER_RUNNER_ID": "cloudflare-worker",
  },

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
  },
}
